## 13. What NOT to build

This is important because we have limited time.

| Don't build                       | Build instead                           |
| --------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| 20 different AI agents            | 7 genuinely useful agents               |
| Complex blockchain integration    | Transparent internal ledger             |
| Real crypto wallets               | PayPal Sandbox                          |
| Custom LLM training               | Existing LLM with tool calling          |
| Fully distributed microservices   | Modular monolith                        |
| A complicated agent framework     | Simple AEP protocol                     |
| Fake agent-to-agent conversations | Actual task execution and contracts     |
| Fake transaction animations       | Actual PayPal authorization and capture |

### Three technical risks to watch

1\. LLM unpredictability

Agents may return invalid bids, exceed budgets or fail tasks.

Solution: Zod validation, retries, timeouts and backend-controlled economic rules.

2\. Payment authorization

Don't capture money simply because an AI says the work is complete.

Solution: independent verification, customer acceptance, PayPal API confirmation and idempotent payment processing.

3\. Subcontracting complexity

Agents could create infinite delegation loops.

Solution: maximum delegation depth of two, budget reservations, cycle detection and concurrent-task limits.

## 14. How to make this more than a hackathon project

There is a larger opportunity here.

AGENT ZERO could eventually become a protocol and infrastructure layer for autonomous commercial agents.

Imagine external developers registering their own agents.

A developer could publish:

```
{
  "agent_name": "LegalResearchPro",
  "capabilities": [
    "legal_document_research",
    "contract_summarization"
  ],
  "pricing": {
    "model": "per_task",
    "minimum": 2.00,
    "maximum": 25.00
  },
  "subcontracting": true
}
```

Another agent could discover and hire it without knowing how it was implemented.

That creates a network effect:

- More registered agents → more capabilities.
- More capabilities → more useful tasks.
- More completed work → stronger reputation.
- Stronger reputation → more commercial opportunities.

Longer term, we could explore interoperability with existing agent communication standards instead of making AEP a closed ecosystem.

## 15. Final implementation priorities

My recommendation is to organize development around these five non-negotiable demonstrations:

1\. Autonomous agent discovery

An agent can find another agent based on capabilities, pricing and reliability.

2\. Economic negotiation

Agents can submit bids, negotiate and create actual contracts.

3\. Agent-to-agent subcontracting

An agent can hire another agent to complete a real part of its assigned task.

4\. Verifiable execution

Agents deliver evidence and their work is checked before acceptance.

5\. PayPal-powered economic settlement

A real Sandbox payment authorization and capture, followed by transparent internal agent accounting.

